<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Portable auto-update CORE for the OpenBurnBar Windows port
    (Phase 5 · W10 · R19). This is the PLATFORM-INDEPENDENT security crux of the
    Windows auto-updater: the update-feed parser (Sparkle/WinSparkle appcast XML
    + the latest-windows.json companion), the Ed25519 PIN-VERIFY over the
    downloaded artifact bytes, the SHA-256 integrity check, the monotonic version
    comparator (downgrade-blocking), and the fail-closed decision tree that
    rejects on ANY mismatch.

    It mirrors the SHIPPED macOS behavior (Sparkle Ed25519-signed appcast +
    custom in-app updater): the feed key is PINNED (Ed25519, RFC 8032)
    INDEPENDENT of the Authenticode signing certificate. An attacker who can
    tamper the feed transport, swap the artifact, or even re-sign with the
    Authenticode cert still cannot ship a payload the pinned feed key did not
    sign (R19). Parity sources:
      * scripts/generate-macos-appcast.mjs  (the appcast.xml + latest-macos.json feed format)
      * Sparkle's `sparkle:edSignature` semantics (Ed25519 over the enclosure bytes)

    It has NO Windows / WinUI / P/Invoke dependency on purpose — the SAME managed
    assembly is:
      * consumed by the net8.0-windows host adapter
        (OpenBurnBar.Updater.Windows) whose WinSparkle / Squirrel integration
        drives the OS-native update UX behind the IUpdaterHost seam, AND
      * unit-tested on the macOS authoring host today via `dotnet test`
        (windows/tests/updater) — exactly the pattern proven by the storage,
        computeruse, and cloudsync cores.

    Ed25519 (RFC 8032) is provided by the pure-managed, cross-platform
    BouncyCastle.Cryptography package so the pin-verify is REAL crypto on both
    the macOS authoring host and Windows CI (the .NET BCL has no built-in
    Ed25519). SHA-256 uses the BCL. The verify lives behind the
    IUpdateSignatureVerifier seam so a host can swap the primitive without
    touching the feed / version / decision logic.

    Targets net8.0 so the net8.0-windows adapter can reference it, while the
    net10.0 test project consumes it too (a lower TFM is usable by a higher one).
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>disable</ImplicitUsings>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <RootNamespace>OpenBurnBar.Updater.Core</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>OpenBurnBar.Updater.Core</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="BouncyCastle.Cryptography" Version="2.4.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
